# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the normalized ASan lane.
#
# Use:  LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=<repo>/lsan.supp
# (NOT ASAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=… — the ASan parser accepts only
#  interceptor_name / interceptor_via_fun / … and refuses `leak:` lines).
#
# Both families below are process-resident by design: allocated once,
# never freed, and outlive any ownership the project could express.
# NONE of them is a project-owned object on the project heap. Verified:
# every leak block in a full ASan+LSan run roots in one of these two
# runtimes; zero stacks root in project domain code.

# --- Family A: Dobby inline-hook trampolines ------------------------------
# herd::install_all() (patches/herd_patch.cpp) installs one DobbyHook per
# patched symbol. Dobby builds a trampoline + indirect stub in near-exec
# memory for each and keeps it for the process lifetime.
# Empirically the same 7474 bytes / 69 allocations report on every
# binary that installs the hooks, regardless of program behavior.
leak:DobbyHook
leak:dobby-src

# --- Family B: ROCm / HSA runtime one-time singletons ---------------------
# libhsa-runtime64 and libamdhip64 build process-wide tables on first use
# (pthread_once paths, device-info caches). Frames are stripped, so match
# by shared-library name — the most stable selector available.
leak:libhsa-runtime64.so.1
leak:libamdhip64.so.7

# Deliberately absent: `leak:herd_patch.cpp` or any project file/module.
# A file-level suppression would swallow future real leaks from the same
# translation unit and hide exactly the defects this lane exists to catch.
